{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["The scepter shall not depart from Yehudah — the kingship that was given to him, that all twelve of his brothers would bow down to him, shall not cease from him, all that greatness — nor a lawgiver — and authority from his seed — until he comes to Shiloh — that is, until there comes the king of Yehudah, namely Rehoboam son of Solomon, who came to renew the kingship at Shiloh, which is near Shechem. But then the ten tribes would depart from him and they would make Jeroboam king, and nothing would remain for Rehoboam son of Solomon except Yehudah and Binyamin." "To him shall the obedience of the peoples — the gathering of the nations who were subject under Solomon his father, as it is written: הוא רודה בכל עבר הנהר — they gathered there to make Rehoboam king, as it is written: וילך רחבעם שכמה כי שכם בא כל ישראל להמליך אותו, and Shechem is near Shiloh, as it is written in Joshua: ויאסף יהושע את כל ישראל שכמה, and the entire passage. And ultimately it explicates all these things: היה לפני ה׳ בשילה. And also in Judges: הנה חג ה׳ בשילה במסלה העולה בית אל שכמה וגו׳. And also in Jeremiah: ויבאו אנשים משכם משילה ומשמרון. And there was a level field at Shechem around the terebinth that was with Shechem, suitable for gathering people there, and for the honor of the Tabernacle that was at Shiloh nearby. And Yaakov explicated the essence of Yehudah's greatness, which spanned from David to Rehoboam; but the diminution of that greatness he did not wish to explicate explicitly — you infer it from the implication of the verse, that from Shiloh onward it was diminished."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:47:45.031222895Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}